O(1)

for the iterative version.

Important Requirement

⚠️ Binary Search requires a sorted array.

Correct:

[2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23]

Incorrect:

[12, 2, 23, 5, 8]

If the array is unsorted, Binary Search may give incorrect results.

Recursive Binary Search

Binary Search can also be written using recursion.

def binary_search(arr, left, right, target):
    if left > right:
        return -1

    mid = (left + right) // 2

    if arr[mid] == target:
        return mid

    elif arr[mid] < target:
        return binary_search(arr, mid + 1, right, target)

    else:
        return binary_search(arr, left, mid - 1, target)


arr = [2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56]

print(binary_search(arr, 0, len(arr) - 1, 23))
Iterative vs Recursive
Feature	Iterative	Recursive
Uses loop	Yes	No
Uses recursion	No	Yes
Extra space	O(1)	O(log n)
Easy for beginners	Yes	Slightly harder
Key Takeaways
Binary Search works only on a sorted array.
It repeatedly divides the search space into half.
It uses left, right, and mid.
Worst-case time complexity is O(log n).
Iterative space complexity is O(1).
It is much faster than Linear Search for large sorted data.
